	markdownService := markdown.NewService(
		markdown.WithTagExtension(),
		markdown.WithMentionExtension(),
	)
	return &APIV1Service{
		...
		MarkdownService:          markdownService,
```

That reaches runtime via `server/server.go:76` (`apiv1.NewAPIV1Service(...)`). Test code builds its own instances — `server/router/api/v1/test/test_helper.go:44` (tags + mentions), `server/router/fileserver/fileserver_test.go:427` (tags only), and `server/router/rss/rss_test.go:54` (no extensions) — but nothing in production path.

**Holders.**

- `APIV1Service.MarkdownService` (`server/router/api/v1/v1.go:35`) — used for mention extraction (`server/router/api/v1/memo_mention_helpers.go:32`), webhook snippets (`server/router/api/v1/memo_service_webhooks.go:85`), and passed into `memopayload.RebuildMemoPayload` on memo create/update (`server/router/api/v1/memo_service.go:108` and `:447`).
- `RSSService.MarkdownService` (`server/router/rss/rss.go:45`, set in `NewRSSService` at `:58`) — calls `RenderHTML` for feed item bodies (`server/router/rss/rss.go:339`). It does not build its own; `server/server.go:85` passes the same instance: `rss.NewRSSService(s.Profile, s.Store, apiV1Service.MarkdownService)`. So RSS and the API share one object.
- `memopayload.Runner.MarkdownService` (`server/runner/memopayload/runner.go:16`, set in `NewRunner` at `:19`) — this holder is currently dead code: `NewRunner` has no callers (`startBackgroundRunners` in `server/server.go` only wires up `s3presign`). The live path is the package-level function `RebuildMemoPayload(ctx, memo, markdownService)` (`server/runner/memopayload/runner.go:74`), which takes the interface as a parameter rather than holding it.

The frontend does its own markdown rendering with markdown-it under `web/src/components/MemoContent/`, which is independent of this Go interface — the interface comment notes the backend `RenderHTML` exists mainly for RSS and other server-side rendering.
